-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **High Compression:** At very high compression ratios (e.g., $r=0.05$, 20× compression), thin structures and grass may not be fully recovered, requiring over 8K Gaussians even with refinement [S1, unofficial].
-- **Empty Clusters:** If a cluster becomes empty during updates, the centroid should be retained or re-initialized to avoid collapse [G].
-- **Zero Norm Tokens:** Tokens with zero norm should be handled to avoid division by zero during normalization [G].
-- **Initialization Sensitivity:** Complexity-weighted initialization provides better quality (0.3 dB gain at $r=0.25$) compared to strided initialization [S1, unofficial].
+## Subtopics
+
+### [K-Means++ Initialization](18746-k-means-initialization.md)
+K-Means++ Initialization is a strategy for selecting initial cluster centers in k-means clustering that ensures the centers are relatively spread out across the data space. It addresses the problem that standard random initialization can lead to exponential runtime and locally optimal solutions far from the global optimum. By selecting subsequent centers with probability proportional to their squared distance from existing centers, it improves theoretical guarantees for both efficiency and clustering quality.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** The k-means cost scales with $Tokens \times Clusters \times Iterations$. At 192 views with $r=0.5$, k-means iterations add 4.2 seconds over full capacity, making it the dominant overhead at high view counts [S1, unofficial]. Convergence typically takes 17–45 iterations for large budgets, but fewer (2–9) for typical compression ratios [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Reduces the number of Gaussian parameters stored by a factor of $1/r$. The scene tokens and assignments are significantly smaller than the full set of geometry tokens [S1, unofficial].
-- **Performance:** At $r=0.5$, storage is halved and rendering speed nearly doubles compared to full capacity [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Adjustable compression at inference without retraining; preserves cross-view consistency (91% of clusters span at least two views at $r=0.1$ and 6 views) [S1, unofficial]. Geometry tokens are the best clustering space, outperforming backbone tokens, patch colors, and even ground-truth 3D points [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** High compression (20×) fails to recover fine details like grass [S1, unofficial]. K-means overhead can be significant at very high view counts [S1, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** The cost of k-means scales with $Tokens \times Clusters \times Iterations$. At 192 views with $r=0.5$, k-means iterations add 4.2 s over full capacity, making it the dominant overhead at high view counts [S1, unofficial]. At 128 views, k-means needs 17–45 iterations to converge, costing 1.9 s at 524K Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** Reduces Gaussian memory footprint by storing only $K$ centroids and labels instead of $N \cdot T$ tokens. At $r=0.5$, storage is halved [S1, unofficial].
+- **Performance:** At $r=0.1$ and 6 views, 91% of geometry token clusters span at least two input views, matching the cross-view consistency of clustering ground-truth 3D points directly [S1, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Allows adjustable compression at inference without retraining. Preserves cross-view consistency better than clustering in other spaces (e.g., backbone tokens, patch colors) [S1, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** High compression (e.g., 20×, $r=0.05$) fails to fully recover thin structures and grass, which require over 8K Gaussians even with TokenBA refinement [S1, unofficial]. The clustering space choice dominates quality: geometry tokens outperform backbone tokens (-0.7 dB), patch colors (-3.7 dB), image positions (-1.0 dB), and even ground-truth 3D points (-0.1 dB) at $r=0.1$ [S1, unofficial].
+- **When to use:** When memory or bandwidth is constrained, and cross-view consistency is important.
+- **When not to use:** When rendering fine, thin structures at very high compression ratios ($r < 0.1$) [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Inference-Time Clustering is part of the ZipSplat method, which aims to compress Gaussian Splatting parameters. It was introduced in the paper "ZipSplat: Fewer Gaussians, Better Splats" [S1, unofficial]. The method leverages the redundancy of geometry tokens across multiple views to merge them into a smaller set of scene tokens. It builds on the idea of token merging in vision transformers but applies it to 3D Gaussian Splatting parameters. The use of spherical k-means and complexity-weighted initialization is specific to this application to preserve fine details and cross-view consistency.
+Inference-Time Clustering was introduced in the ZipSplat paper [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the idea that many patches observe the same surface from different viewpoints, carrying redundant information that can be merged. The method was evaluated on DL3DV and RealEstate10K datasets [S1, unofficial].
 
